===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*solh₂-}}
From {{confix|en|holo|pos1=from {{der|en|grc|ὅλος||whole}}|on|pos2=like {{m|en|taxon}} or {{m|en|meron}}|id2=unit}}. {{coin|en|Arthur Koestler}} in his 1967 book ''{{w|The Ghost in the Machine}}''.

====Noun====
{{examples|An individual is autonomous, but also part of a family, which is part of an extended family, which is part of a community, etc.}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|philosophy}} Something that is both a [[part]] and a [[whole]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Ken Wilber|title=w:Sex, Ecology, Spirituality|year=1995|publisher=Shambhala|chapter=The Pattern That Connects|section=Book One|pages=33–34|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sexecologyspirit00wilb/page/33/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-57062-072-0|passage=Before an atom is an atom, it is a '''holon'''. Before a cell is a cell, it is a '''holon'''. Before an idea is an idea, it is a '''holon'''. All of them are wholes that exist in other wholes, and thus they are all whole/parts, or '''holons''', first and foremost (long before any “particular characteristics” are singled out by us).}}

=====Usage notes=====
Used primarily in philosophy (where the term originated), family therapy and in manufacturing.

